 ## Background
-Sorting splats by depth is a foundational requirement for correct alpha blending in object-order volume rendering. Early implementations relied on CPU-side sorting due to the limited parallel sorting capabilities of early GPUs [S2, unofficial]. As GPU compute shaders and fragment shader networks matured, data-independent algorithms like Bitonic Sort became viable for real-time rendering, allowing the GPU to handle the sorting overhead entirely [S1, unofficial]. Modern approaches often combine these techniques, using CPU sorting for single-renderer scenes and GPU-based cascaded merges for multi-renderer or large-scale scenes [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+Originating from the need to resolve order-dependent transparency in splat-based volume rendering, these algorithms evolved alongside 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Early implementations relied on CPU sorting due to GPU limitations, while modern approaches leverage GPU subgroups and merge networks for real-time global sorting [S1, unofficial].
 
